### Why streaming evaluation?

The model predicts gaze **continuously at every timestep** from causal
(past-only) context, instead of once per whole window — this is required by
Akida's `BufferTempConv` primitive, the only supported hardware path for 1D
temporal convolution on Akida 2 (the same primitive used by BrainChip's own
eye-tracking reference model, `akida_models/tenn_spatiotemporal/eye_train.py`).
A symmetric Conv1D-as-Conv2D reshape was tried first and trains/quantizes
fine, but `cnn2snn.convert()` rejects it (Akida's 2D conv hardware only
supports square kernels). Early timesteps in a window have little causal
context ("warm-up") and are less accurate — see the plot above and
`docs/benchmark_report.md` for the full account, including a second
deployable model (a Tiny MLP baseline, not part of this standard pipeline).

Because Akida's buffered/FIFO layers only maintain correct state for
`batch_size=1`, evaluation and benchmarking both feed one independent window
at a time, sequentially — see `eyetracking_eval.py` and
`eyetracking_benchmark.py`.

The model itself: causal Conv3D(16, k=5) → BN → ReLU → causal Conv3D(32, k=3)
→ BN → ReLU → Dense(32) → ReLU → Dense(2), with the first two Conv3D layers
becoming `BufferTempConv` after quantization. **3,490 parameters.**

## Requirements

For environment requirements and setup, see the [Requirements](../../../README.md#requirements)
section of the top-level README. This example additionally needs `pandas`
(GazeBase CSV loading) and `matplotlib` (figures).

## Dataset

Gaze is simulated from real eye-movement trajectories rather than recorded
with real photodiode hardware — no public dataset of raw photodiode signals
with gaze ground truth exists (confirmed across GazeBase, GazeBaseVR,
JuDo1000, TEyeD, the Texas State PS-OG dataset, and every real
photodiode-hardware paper found; full survey in `docs/dataset_report.md`).
This example uses:

- **GazeBase** (Griffith et al. 2021, CC BY 4.0, 322 subjects) as the real
  gaze-trajectory source.
- A simulated 8-channel PSOG/photodiode response (`data/psog_simulate.py`),
  following the Gaussian-windowed virtual-sensor model from Rigas, Raffle &
  Komogortsev (arXiv:1707.05413), applied to GazeBase's `Random_Saccades`
  task across both sessions, all 322 Round_1 subjects.
- A 64-timestep sliding window (stride 32) over the 8-channel signal; labels
  are the per-timestep (x, y) gaze angle in degrees, not a single
  end-of-window value (see "Why streaming evaluation" above).
- Subject-disjoint splits: 225 train / 48 val / 49 test subjects, no subject
  in more than one split.

This is a real, honest proxy for validating the pipeline and model
architecture, not a substitute for real photodiode hardware data — read
numbers here as "does the approach work," not "this is what an Akida
deployment will see in the wild."

## Pipeline

| Stage | Description |
|---|---|
| Full-precision | Float32 training from scratch (regression, MSE loss on z-normalized targets) |
| 8-bit quantization | `quantizeml quantize` to 8-bit weights and activations (8-bit input), calibrated on real samples; no QAT required |
| Conversion to Akida | `cnn2snn convert` to Akida 2 format (quantizeml's `replace_conv3d` bufferizes the Conv3D layers into `BufferTempConv` automatically) |

## Dataset setup

Unlike the image-classification examples in this zoo, there is no single
tarball to download — building the dataset requires the raw GazeBase
recordings plus a preprocessing/simulation step:

```bash
# 1. Download GazeBase_v2_0.zip from figshare (CC BY 4.0, no request form):
#    https://doi.org/10.6084/m9.figshare.12912257
mkdir -p data/raw
mv GazeBase_v2_0.zip data/raw/

# 2. Build the per-timestep windowed dataset (train/val/test _seq.npz)
python data/build_dataset_seq.py data/raw/GazeBase_v2_0.zip
```

This writes `data/processed/{{train,val,test}}_seq.npz`, which
`eyetracking_data.get_data()` / `get_samples()` read. Both the raw zip and
the generated `.npz` files are git-ignored (large, regeneratable).

## Reference Models

Pretrained models are made available here, within the `pretrained_models/`
folder. However, those are handled using the `git-lfs` package (git large
file storage). For those to be downloaded with the repo, you will need to
set up `git-lfs`. For further instructions, see the
[Trained models](../../../README.md#trained-models) section of the top-level README.

## Usage

### Notebook

Two notebooks are provided that walk through a) preparation of a trained
Akida-compatible model and b) evaluation and benchmarking of that model on
Akida.

[eyetracking_notebook_training.ipynb](eyetracking_notebook_training.ipynb)
walks through the complete training pipeline end-to-end, including why the
model is causal/streaming and what the `BufferTempConv` conversion means in
practice.

[![Open In Colab](https://colab.research.google.com/assets/colab-badge.svg)](https://colab.research.google.com/github/Brainchip-Inc/brainchip_devhub/blob/main/akida2/model_zoo/eyetracking/eyetracking_notebook_training.ipynb)

[eyetracking_notebook_benchmark.ipynb](eyetracking_notebook_benchmark.ipynb)
walks through evaluation of model accuracy on Akida and, if a hardware
device is available, benchmarking of model latency.

> **Note:** the hardware benchmark section requires a physical Akida 2 FPGA
> platform with a connected board.

### Script

For straightforward reproduction of the training and evaluation results
(once the dataset is built — see "Dataset setup" above), run the full
pipeline in one shot:

```bash
bash eyetracking_train.sh [DATADIR]
```

The optional `DATADIR` argument overrides the default processed-dataset
location (`./data/processed`).

## Project history

This example is the standard-interface distillation of a broader research
project exploring camera-less eye tracking on Akida, including a Tiny MLP
baseline (also Akida-deployable, no architecture rework needed — see
`docs/benchmark_report.md`), a non-causal CNN variant (not Akida-deployable,
kept for the dataset-quality/shift-robustness findings it produced), and a
shift-robustness study (per-session calibration recovers most of the
accuracy lost to sensor misalignment — see `README_project.md` and
`docs/` for the full write-up). That history is preserved under `data/`,
`models/`, `training/`, `quantize/`, and `deploy/` for reference; the
top-level `eyetracking_*.py` scripts and this README are the clean,
standard zoo-example entry point built from the one model that actually
deploys end-to-end on Akida.

## Contributing and Maintenance

This README is autogenerated from `docs/README.md.template`
so that the accuracy and hardware benchmark values are written directly
by the code (via the `metrics.json` file, also in the docs folder).

When the associated model or training pipeline is modified to improve
performance, you should rerun the evaluations of the float and quantized
model versions, plus the hardware benchmark, including the
`--save-metrics` argument, and then regenerate the README from the template
using `update_readme.py`:
```bash
# Float model
python eyetracking_eval.py -l pretrained_models/tinytemporalcnn_eyetracking.h5 --save-metrics

# 8-bit variant
python eyetracking_eval.py -l pretrained_models/tinytemporalcnn_eyetracking_i8_w8_a8.h5 --save-metrics
python eyetracking_eval.py -l pretrained_models/tinytemporalcnn_eyetracking_i8_w8_a8.fbz --save-metrics
python eyetracking_benchmark.py -l pretrained_models/tinytemporalcnn_eyetracking_i8_w8_a8.fbz --save-metrics

python update_readme.py
```
Then commit the changed files (template, metrics and updated README).

Likewise, if you want to edit the contents of this README, you should
not edit it directly, but instead edit `docs/README.md.template` and
then regenerate the README using
```bash
python update_readme.py
```
